abstract: >
  Reliable in-season maize (Zea mays L.) yield prediction is critical for
  precision crop management, but conventional approaches often struggle to
  capture the dynamic interactions between crop development and environmental
  conditions. This study developed a Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) deep
  learning model that integrates Unmanned Aerial Vehicle (UAV)-based
  multispectral imagery with multi-source environmental data to predict maize
  yield across different growth phases. UAV observations were collected from
  three maize fields in Temple, Texas, during the 2022–2023 growing seasons,
  spanning the third-leaf stage through physiological maturity. Individual
  growth stages were grouped into five broader growth phases, specifically
  early, mid, and late vegetative, flowering, and mid-reproductive, and fused
  with soil data, weather data (WD), and structural information (SI). The LSTM
  model was evaluated across 248 combinations of growth-phase sequences and
  data configurations. Results demonstrated that spectral features (SF) alone
  provided strong predictive power (R² = 0.74–0.89) on held-out test data,
  particularly during the late vegetative and mid-reproductive growth phases.
  However, integrating SF with SI and WD further enhanced prediction accuracy,
  achieving R² = 0.91 and RMSE = 0.93 Mg ha⁻¹ for the late vegetative to
  flowering sequence. Analysis of feature importance using SHapley Additive
  exPlanations (SHAP) revealed red-edge band, near-infrared band, enhanced
  vegetation index, canopy height, soil electrical conductivity at 30 cm
  depth, and precipitation as important predictors, with their relative
  importance varying by growth phase and data-fusion combination. This study
  establishes a robust and scalable approach for high-resolution, in-season
  maize yield prediction by integrating UAV-based multispectral data,
  multi-source data fusion, and deep learning, enabling reliable forecasting
  and informed crop management in precision agriculture.

